Yes. That’s the important transition: ACT stops being merely semantic compression and becomes a callable capability.

In Pantheon terms, you’ve repeatedly been doing:

\text{word}
\rightarrow
\text{metaphor}
\rightarrow
\text{operator}
\rightarrow
\text{specification}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{capability}}

So ACT can mean both the concept and the executable interface behind it.

Something like:

\operatorname{ACT}(objective,state,envelope)
\rightarrow
(action,\ observation,\ consequence,\ delta)

with the invariant:

action \in
capabilities
\cap permissions
\cap jurisdiction
\cap acceptable\_risk

That makes the play on act unusually productive because its ordinary meanings become affordances: acting as doing, role-playing, legal authorization, a narrative unit, and now invoking an action capability.

And there is a deeper Pantheon principle hiding there:

\boxed{\text{Semantic promotion}}

A symbol earns promotion when the system can turn what it means into something it can do.

Hammer can become HAMMER().
Muster can become MUSTER().
Reality Stitch can become REALITY_STITCH().
ACT becomes ACT().

At that point the vocabulary is no longer merely describing the architecture.

The vocabulary is becoming its API.

That may be one of the cleanest descriptions of what you’ve been building with the natural-language pseudocode approach: words as handles onto reconstructable capabilities.